      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import scipy.stats as stats"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Section 3.1.1. Python module `scipy.stats` and methods for Statistical Distributions\n",
        "According to the [SciPy main manual](https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/tutorial/stats.html), the `scipy.stats` module provides unified access to the main theoretical distributions used in mathematical statistics (normal, *t*-, *χ*²-, *F*-distributions, binomial, Poisson, *etc.*), as well as access to the corresponding distribution functions, densities, quantile functions, and the procedures for generating the random numbers. This allows us to focus on formulating and interpreting statistical problems without being distracted by low-level details.\n",
        "\n",
        "The module's interface is uniform. Each distribution is represented by an object with a set of standard methods (`pdf`/`pmf`, `cdf`, `ppf`, `rvs`, *etc.*). Thanks to this, learning how to work with one distribution is easily transferred to the others, which makes further calculations and code more transparent and easier to maintain.\n",
        "\n",
        "`scipy.stats` is tightly integrated with `NumPy`. All operations are performed on `ndarray` arrays and support vectorisation.\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "d6-lbfZRER9O"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "All continuous distributions in SciPy use the same parameter scheme:\n",
        "\n",
        "`loc` — shift (usually the mean or mathematical expectation *μ*),\n",
        "\n",
        "`scale` — scale (usually the standard deviation *σ*, not the variance),\n",
        "\n",
        "`size` — the shape of the random value array (analogous to the dimensions in MATLAB).\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "QxUnbIlJR7ga"
      }
    },
